{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788201281,"nonce":"yYgsOLeOgOqyGfKWw8QkZF1YPO8=","message":"VERIFICATION (backlog verification pass, git-bug 2f2796e, run fbb2c59)\n\nCORRECTED — one sub-claim is wrong by member, and the ACCEPTANCE SECTION STILL\nCONTRADICTS AMENDMENT #1. Everything else in the recon (comment #3) re-verified\nclean, member by member, and the amendments are unusually accurate. Detail below,\nconfirmations first so the corrections are not read as a rejection.\n\n=====================================================================\nPART 1 — CONFIRMED BY READING (all against RimWorldBase, by member)\n=====================================================================\n\nLETTERS\n\n- `Verse/NewQuestLetter.Choices` yields only `Option_ViewInQuestsTab(\"ViewQuest\")`,\n  `Option_JumpToLocation`, `Option_Close`; `NewQuestLetter.OpenLetter` does\n  `SetCurrentTab(MainButtonDefOf.Quests)` / `.Select(quest)` /\n  `Find.LetterStack.RemoveLetter(this)`. **No accept option.** CONFIRMED.\n- `RimWorld/ChoiceLetter_GrowthMoment` extends `LetterWithTimeout`, NOT\n  `ChoiceLetter` — so it has no `Choices` member at all, exactly as the recon\n  says. `OpenLetter` is `TrySetChoices(); ... new Dialog_GrowthMomentChoices(text, this)`.\n  `MakeChoices(List\u003cSkillDef\u003e, Trait)` is public. CONFIRMED.\n  And `TrySetChoices` writes `passionChoices`/`traitChoices`, both scribed via\n  `Scribe_Collections.Look` in `ExposeData`, using `PassionOptions(...).InRandomOrder()`,\n  `Rand.Value \u003c 0.35f` and `PawnGenerator.GenerateTraitsFor`. So \"its open path\n  rolls the shared Rand into scribed fields\" — CONFIRMED, and it is on\n  `OpenLetter` itself, i.e. unavoidable if the letter is ever opened.\n- `Verse/BundleLetter.OpenLetter` is `if (Event.current.button == 0) Find.WindowStack.Add(new FloatMenu(floatMenuOptions));`.\n  `Event.current` is null outside OnGUI, so calling it from tick context NREs on\n  `.button` before it ever reaches the FloatMenu. CONFIRMED, including the\n  `Event.current` hazard.\n- `Verse/DeathLetter` overrides `OpenLetter` and keeps the contract (`Choices` =\n  Close / ReadMore / ViewInQuestsTab, assembled into a `DiaNode`). CONFIRMED.\n- The override set really is five: `Verse/ChoiceLetter` (the base implementation),\n  `NewQuestLetter`, `DeathLetter`, `BundleLetter`, `ChoiceLetter_GrowthMoment`.\n  A grep for `override void OpenLetter` returns exactly those files. CONFIRMED.\n- `Verse/LetterStack.OpenAutomaticLetters` opens with\n  `if (Find.WindowStack.WindowsForcePause) { return; }`. CONFIRMED.\n- `Verse/LetterStack.BundleLetter` (the getter) is\n  `if (bundleLetterCache == null) bundleLetterCache = (BundleLetter)LetterMaker.MakeLetter(LetterDefOf.BundleLetter);`,\n  `Verse/LetterMaker.MakeLetter(LetterDef)` assigns\n  `obj.ID = Find.UniqueIDsManager.GetNextLetterID()`, and\n  `RimWorld/UniqueIDsManager.ExposeData` has\n  `Scribe_Values.Look(ref nextLetterID, \"nextLetterID\", 0)`.\n  Write-on-read on a SCRIBED counter. CONFIRMED end to end.\n- `LetterStackUpdate` reaps by `if (!letters[num].CanShowInLetterStack) RemoveLetter(...)`\n  and is frame-driven. CONFIRMED.\n\nDIALOGS\n\n- `Verse/DiaOption.Activate()` is `protected` and contains no `disabled` test.\n  The gate is one line up, in `DiaOption.OptOnGUI`:\n  `else if (Widgets.ButtonText(rect, text, drawBackground: false, !disabled, textColor, active \u0026\u0026 !disabled))`\n  and only then `Activate();`. CONFIRMED — and it is even more literally \"the gate\n  is an argument to a Widgets call\" than the recon says: `disabled` appears TWICE\n  in that argument list.\n- `DiaOption.text` is `protected string text;` with a public `SetText` and no\n  getter; `Dialog_NodeTree.curNode` and `.title` are `protected`. Field-refs\n  needed. CONFIRMED.\n- `Verse/Dialog_NodeTree` constructor sets `forcePause = true;` and\n  `closeOnCancel = false;`. `GotoNode(DiaNode)` is public. CONFIRMED, including\n  \"escape is not what clears them\".\n\nTRADE\n\n- `RimWorld/TradeSession` is a static class; `SetupWith` touches no window;\n  `Close()` is `trader = null;` and nothing else. CONFIRMED.\n- `TradeSession.Close()` has ZERO callers in the tree (grep for `TradeSession.Close()`\n  returns nothing). `TradeSession.SetupWith` has exactly ONE: `RimWorld/Dialog_Trade`.\n  CONFIRMED, and that is the proof of the recon's claim that the window owned the\n  lifecycle.\n- The NRE. `RimWorld/TradeDeal.TryExecute`, verbatim:\n\n      if (CurrencyTradeable == null || CurrencyTradeable.CountPostDealFor(Transactor.Colony) \u003c 0)\n      {\n          Find.WindowStack.WindowOfType\u003cDialog_Trade\u003e().FlashSilver();\n\n  CONFIRMED, and the recon's proposed pre-check is EXACTLY the negation of that\n  condition, so it makes the branch unreachable. Note the gift branch returns\n  before it, so gift mode never reaches the NRE.\n- The whole trade model is window-free apart from that one line. I grepped\n  `Find.WindowStack`, `Dialog_Trade`, `Find.UIRoot` and `Event.current` across\n  `TradeDeal`, `TradeSession`, `Tradeable`, `Tradeable_Pawn`, `TradeUtility` and\n  `FactionGiftUtility`: two hits total, `TradeDeal.TryExecute` (the NRE) and\n  `FactionGiftUtility.OfferGiftsCommand` (a caravan gizmo, not on our path).\n  `FactionGiftUtility.GiveGift` itself is clean. CONFIRMED.\n- Trade is a job, on both routes. `RimWorld/Building_CommsConsole.GiveUseCommsJob`\n  is `JobMaker.MakeJob(JobDefOf.UseCommsConsole, this); job.commTarget = target; negotiator.jobs.TryTakeOrderedJob(job, JobTag.Misc);`.\n  `RimWorld/FloatMenuOptionProvider_Trade` is\n  `JobMaker.MakeJob(JobDefOf.TradeWithPawn, clickedPawn); job.playerForced = true; ... TryTakeOrderedJob(...)`.\n  CONFIRMED — order-then-advance-then-transact.\n- `Building_CommsConsole.CanUseCommsNow` is the electricity-disabled game\n  condition, then `powerComp.PowerOn`. CONFIRMED.\n- `RimWorld.Planet/Settlement_TraderTracker.StockListForReading` is\n  `if (stock == null || stock.InnerListForReading.Empty()) { RegenerateStock(); } return stock.InnerListForReading;`.\n  CONFIRMED, and the scope note is right: it is the world-map settlement tracker,\n  off our path.\n- `RimWorld/Tradeable` reads the static session unguarded in several members —\n  e.g. `public virtual bool TraderWillTrade =\u003e TradeSession.trader.TraderKind.WillTrade(ThingDef);`\n  — so it NREs with no session. CONFIRMED.\n\nCOMMS AND QUESTS\n\n- `RimWorld/FactionDialogMaker`: `RequestTraderOption`, `RequestOrbitalTraderOption`,\n  `RequestMilitaryAidOption` and `CallForAid` are all `private static`, and the\n  effects live inside `diaOption.action = delegate {...}`. The bodies really are\n  about three lines: the caravan one is\n  `Find.Storyteller.incidentQueue.Add(IncidentDefOf.TraderCaravanArrival, TicksGame + 120000, parms, 240000)`;\n  the orbital one adds `IncidentDefOf.OrbitalTraderArrival`, sets\n  `faction.lastOrbitalTraderRequestTick` and calls\n  `TryAffectGoodwillWith(faction, -30, ...)`; `CallForAid` ends at\n  `IncidentDefOf.RaidFriendly.Worker.TryExecute(incidentParms)`. CONFIRMED.\n- `RimWorld/FactionUIUtility` adds only `Dialog_InfoCard`; `RimWorld/MainTabWindow_Factions`\n  has no button at all. Read-only. CONFIRMED — do not spec a factions-tab verb.\n- `RimWorld/Faction.TryAffectGoodwillWith(Faction, int, bool, bool, HistoryEventDef, GlobalTargetInfo?)`\n  exists with that signature. CONFIRMED.\n- `RimWorld/Quest.Accept(Pawn by)`, `Quest.RequiresAccepter`, `Quest.dismissed`,\n  `RimWorld/QuestUtility.CanAcceptQuest(Quest)` returning `AcceptanceReport`,\n  `QuestUtility.CanPawnAcceptQuest(Pawn, Quest)` and\n  `RimWorld/QuestPart_Choice.Choose(Choice)` — all exist, all public, all as\n  described. CONFIRMED.\n  (Method note against myself: a truncated grep briefly told me `CanAcceptQuest`\n  did not exist. It does, in `QuestUtility`, returning an `AcceptanceReport`.\n  Re-run without truncation before believing an absence.)\n- The auto-choose red error, verbatim from `QuestPart_Choice.PreQuestAccept`:\n\n      if (choices.Count \u003e= 2)\n      {\n          Log.Error(\"Tried to accept a quest but \" + GetType().Name + \" still has a choice unresolved. Auto-choosing the first option.\");\n          Choose(choices[0]);\n      }\n\n  CONFIRMED. Choose before accepting, always.\n\n=====================================================================\nPART 2 — THE CORRECTION\n=====================================================================\n\n**\"Safe dismissal is `WindowStack.TryRemove(window, false)` — it copies to temp\nlists, so mid-frame removal is by design.\" The CONCLUSION is right; the REASON is\nwrong by member, and the wrong reason would mislead the worker.**\n\n`Verse/WindowStack.TryRemove` copies nothing. It is:\n\n      if (!window.OnCloseRequest()) { return false; }\n      if (doCloseSound \u0026\u0026 window.soundClose != null) { window.soundClose.PlayOneShotOnCamera(); }\n      window.PreClose();\n      windows.Remove(window);\n      window.PostClose();\n\n— a direct mutation of the live `windows` list. The member that copies is\n`WindowStack.WindowStackOnGUI`, which does\n`windowStackOnGUITmpList.Clear(); windowStackOnGUITmpList.AddRange(windows);`\nbefore iterating. THAT is what makes removal from inside OnGUI safe.\n`WindowStack.WindowsUpdate` iterates `windows` directly and does not copy.\n\nWhy this is a distinction and not pedantry: AutoRimmer's verbs run from\n`GameComponentUpdate`, which is neither of those loops, so `TryRemove` is safe\nhere — but for a DIFFERENT reason than the one recorded. A worker who believes\n\"TryRemove copies\" will also believe it is safe from inside any iteration, which\nis false.\n\nThree things the same read turns up that the recon does not mention, all\nload-bearing for a `dialog dismiss` verb:\n\n- `TryRemove` returns **false** when the window is not on the stack, OR when\n  `Window.OnCloseRequest()` refuses. The default returns `true` and only\n  `RimWorld/Page_ModsConfig` overrides it, so a refusal is unlikely — but the\n  bool must be read and reported, not discarded. A \"dismissed\" that silently did\n  nothing is exactly the failure mode 4087644 is about.\n- `TryRemove` runs `PreClose()` and `PostClose()`. For a node tree that is\n  `Dialog_NodeTree.PreClose -\u003e curNode.PreClose()` and\n  `Dialog_NodeTree.PostClose -\u003e closeAction()`. **`closeAction` is arbitrary game\n  code**, set by whoever built the dialog. Dismissal is not inert, and the verb\n  should say so in its result per the disclosure rule in DESIGN.\n- Only the SOUND is suppressed by `doCloseSound: false`. Nothing else is skipped.\n\n=====================================================================\nPART 3 — THE ACCEPTANCE SECTION IS STALE AND ONE CRITERION IS IMPOSSIBLE\n=====================================================================\n\n**\"Choice letter (quest offer) accepted via verb -\u003e quest active\" cannot be\nsatisfied, and amendment #1 already says so.** `NewQuestLetter` has no accept\noption; accept is `QuestPart_Choice.Choose` plus `Quest.Accept`. The Acceptance\nsection was written before that amendment and was never updated, so a worker\nreading top-down is measured against a criterion the same issue disproves 200\nlines later. Rewrite it before dispatch as, roughly: a quest offer letter is\nlisted and read; `quest accept {quest}` — choosing a reward first where a\n`QuestPart_Choice` exists — makes the quest active; the letter is separately\ndismissed; zero red errors, in particular no \"still has a choice unresolved\".\n\n**\"Dev-spawned trader: buy 10 meals + sell 5 shirts\" is not a fixture that exists\nyet.** `dev:spawn-pawn` produces a pawn, not a trade partner:\n`TradeSession.SetupWith` logs `Log.Warning(\"Called SetupWith with a trader not willing to trade now.\")`\nunless `ITrader.CanTradeNow`, and the map-trader route needs a\n`LordJob_TradeWithColony` (`FloatMenuOptionProvider_Trade` reads\n`clickedPawn.GetLord().LordJob is LordJob_TradeWithColony` for its dismiss\noption). So the fixture is an incident — `IncidentDefOf.TraderCaravanArrival` or\n`OrbitalTraderArrival`, both reachable through the existing `dev:incident` — or a\nnew `dev:` step that builds the lord. Name whichever it is IN the acceptance, or\nthe worker discovers it at acceptance time. Same class of gap as 3.4's fixture\ntable.\n\n**\"cancel leaves state untouched\" needs a definition.** `TradeSession.Close()`\nnulls `trader` and leaves `deal`, `playerNegotiator` and `giftMode` set, so\n\"untouched\" cannot mean the statics. Two things a `trade start` provably does\nbefore any `set`: `TradeSession.SetupWith` sends\n`Messages.Message(\"MessageCannotSellItemsReason\" ...)` when `deal.cannotSellReasons`\nis non-empty — a journaled message, from START — and `TradeDeal.AddAllTradeables`\nruns `Thing thing = ThingMaker.MakeThing(ThingDefOf.Silver);` when the trader has\nno silver tradeable. `Thing.PostMake` calls `ThingIDMaker.GiveIDTo(this)` which is\n`t.thingIDNumber = Find.UniqueIDsManager.GetNextThingID()`, and `nextThingID` is\n`Scribe_Values.Look`-scribed; `PostMake` also rolls\n`def.startingHpRange.RandomInRange`. **So opening a trade session burns a scribed\nthing ID and a Rand call.** Small, unavoidable — it is what the click does — and\ntherefore DISCLOSED rather than suppressed, the same call `orders` made for the\njob-ID counter. Define \"untouched\" as \"no `Tradeable.ResolveTrade` ran; colony\nsilver and stock counts unchanged\", not as \"nothing moved\".\n\n=====================================================================\nPART 4 — FIVE ADDITIONS THE RECON MISSED. NUMBER 1 IS THE EXPENSIVE ONE.\n=====================================================================\n\n**ADDITION 1 — replaying a letter option's `Activate()` body will NRE on the\nDEFAULT path, and choosing an option does NOT close an already-open window.**\nTwo facts compose badly:\n\n  (a) `DiaOption.Activate()` begins `if (resolveTree) { OwningDialog.Close(); }`\n      where `protected Dialog_NodeTree OwningDialog =\u003e (Dialog_NodeTree)dialog;`\n      and the public field `dialog` is assigned in exactly one place —\n      `Dialog_NodeTree.GotoNode`, which does\n      `foreach (DiaOption option in node.options) { option.dialog = this; }`.\n  (b) EVERY stock `ChoiceLetter` option sets `resolveTree = true`: `Option_Close`,\n      `Option_Reject`, `Option_JumpToLocation`, `Option_JumpToLocationAndPostpone`,\n      `Option_Postpone`, `Option_ViewInQuestsTab`. And `ChoiceLetter.Choices` is an\n      ITERATOR whose getters construct a `new DiaOption(...)` on every enumeration.\n\n  So a verb that reads `letter.Choices` and faithfully replays Activate hits\n  `null.Close()` on the FIRST branch, for essentially every vanilla letter. Not an\n  edge case — the default. Guard it and say why in the comment.\n\n  The second half is worse, and it is this spec's whole purpose. Because `Choices`\n  mints fresh objects, the options in an OPEN `Dialog_NodeTreeWithFactionInfo` are\n  different objects from the ones a `letter choose` verb enumerates. Replaying the\n  action removes the LETTER; the WINDOW stays on the stack, still `forcePause`, and\n  `LetterStack.OpenAutomaticLetters` still early-returns. **The 1.7 wedge is not\n  cleared by answering the letter.** `letter choose` must ALSO `TryRemove` the\n  window that letter opened, or the run stays halted at 0 ticks — which is exactly\n  the failure amendment #2 says this spec exists to prevent. Put it in the\n  acceptance: after `letter choose`, `interactions` reports `force_pause {count:0}`\n  and the next `advance` runs its full budget.\n\n**ADDITION 2 — \"replaying the action IS the dismissal\" collides head-on with this\nspec's own \"Zero UI-widget driving\" invariant, for two of the six stock options.**\n`ChoiceLetter.Option_ViewInQuestsTab`'s action is\n`Find.MainTabsRoot.SetCurrentTab(MainButtonDefOf.Quests); ((MainTabWindow_Quests)MainButtonDefOf.Quests.TabWindow).Select(quest); Find.LetterStack.RemoveLetter(this);`\nand `Option_JumpToLocation`'s action is\n`CameraJumper.TryJumpAndSelect(target); Find.LetterStack.RemoveLetter(this);`.\nReplaying those drives a main tab and the camera. The rule that resolves it\ncleanly is already this project's rule for vanilla helpers that drag UI in:\n**reproduce the option's MODEL effect (`RemoveLetter`, plus the quest/faction\nmutations) and drop the presentation lines**, exactly as 3.2 did with the flick\ntutorial modal. Say which options are replayed wholesale and which are\nre-implemented, per option, in the code.\n\n**ADDITION 3 — there is a SECOND widget gate on option activation, and it is\nprivate and real-time.** `Dialog_NodeTree.DrawNode` calls\n`curNode.options[i].OptOnGUI(rect3, InteractiveNow)`, and\n`private bool InteractiveNow =\u003e Time.realtimeSinceStartup \u003e= makeInteractiveAtTime;`\nwith `makeInteractiveAtTime = RealTime.LastRealTime + 1f` when\n`delayInteractivity: true`. That `active` argument is ANDed into the same\n`Widgets.ButtonText` call as `!disabled`, so it is a real gate on pressing the\nbutton. A decision is owed and should be recorded rather than left implicit. My\nrecommendation: **waive it, with the reason stated in the verb's comment** — it is\nan anti-misclick delay rather than a game rule, it is wall-clock rather than\ntick-based, and reproducing it would put real-time dependence into an otherwise\ndeterministic verb. Note that `ChoiceLetter.OpenLetter` and `DeathLetter.OpenLetter`\nboth pass `delayInteractivity: false`, so letters are unaffected either way; the\ngate matters only for callers that pass true.\n\n**ADDITION 4 — `Quest.Accept` is a silent no-op unless the state is right.**\n`public void Accept(Pawn by) { if (State == QuestState.NotYetAccepted) { ... } }`\n— no return value, no complaint. Read `acceptanceTick` / `State` back after the\ncall; same rule as the FSWA bridge's void setter (\"read the write back\"). And the\nwidget gate is bigger than `CanAcceptQuest` alone:\n`MainTabWindow_Quests.AcceptQuestByInterface` refuses with `\"MessageCannotAcceptQuest\"`,\nthen branches on `RequiresAccepter` into a float menu over\n`PawnsFinder.AllMapsCaravansAndTravellingTransporters_Alive_FreeColonists_NoSuspended`\nfiltered by `CanPawnAcceptQuest`, with `\"MessageNoColonistCanAcceptQuest\"` when\nthat set comes back empty. Also worth knowing: the plain Accept button is DRAWN\nONLY when `questPart_Choice == null`, which is the widget-level form of \"choose\nfirst\".\n\n**ADDITION 5 — the comms path has its own widget gate and its own scribed-state\nwrite, neither named in the spec.** The gate is\n`Building_CommsConsole.GetFailureReason(myPawn)`, which rejects on no power\n(`\"CannotUseNoPower\"`), on `!myPawn.health.capacities.CapableOf(PawnCapacityDefOf.Talking)`,\non `!GetCommTargets(myPawn).Any()`, and finally on `!CanUseCommsNow` — that last\none with `Log.Error(myPawn + \" could not use comm console for unknown reason.\")`,\ni.e. a RED ERROR reachable from the vanilla widget itself. Reproduce the earlier\nchecks so that branch is never reached. And both entry points end with a\nknowledge-database write: `PlayerKnowledgeDatabase.KnowledgeDemonstrated(ConceptDefOf.OpeningComms, KnowledgeAmount.Total)`\nin `GiveUseCommsJob`, and `ConceptDefOf.InteractingWithTraders` in\n`FloatMenuOptionProvider_Trade`'s action. No modal, so no wedge, but it is the\nsame family as the flick trap and the standing rule applies: reproduce the gate\nand the effect, drop the tutorial line.\n\n=====================================================================\nPART 5 — SMALLER NOTES\n=====================================================================\n\n- \"`OpenAutomaticLetters` early-returns FOREVER\" is true in effect and imprecise in\n  mechanism: the guard is `WindowsForcePause`, so it resumes the moment the stack\n  clears. That is good news for this spec — it means the dismiss verb genuinely\n  un-wedges the run, rather than also needing to poke the letter stack.\n- The recon comment cites bare line numbers throughout. Every one resolves\n  correctly in the tree on this box and every one is worthless on BORGES, which is\n  the exact rule the repo has. The member names, in the order the recon cites them:\n  `TradeDeal.TryExecute`, `Quest.Accept`, `LetterStack.OpenAutomaticLetters`,\n  `DiaOption.OptOnGUI`. Worth carrying the member names into the dispatch report.\n- `TradeSession.SetupWith`'s `Log.Warning` on `!CanTradeNow` is a yellow, not a red\n  — but the journal records warnings, and a warning that fires on every trade start\n  is noise the run does not need. Pre-check `CanTradeNow`.\n\n=====================================================================\nWHAT I AM INFERRING, NOT CONFIRMING\n=====================================================================\n\n- That `letter choose` on an OPEN letter leaves the window up. I read that\n  `Choices` mints fresh options and that `dialog` is only ever set by `GotoNode`;\n  I did not run it. It is the single most important thing on this list to check on\n  the bench before the design settles: open a timing letter, call `letter choose`,\n  then read `interactions` for `force_pause`.\n- That waiving `InteractiveNow` is the right call. That is a judgement, not a\n  reading.\n- That a `dev:incident` trader arrival produces a `CanTradeNow` partner within a\n  bounded advance. Plausible from the incident worker, unverified.","files":null}]}